  "step1_summary": "Chief complaint: Several days of severe diarrhoea with fever and vomiting. Abdominal pain around the umbilicus, cramping, and episodes of urge to stool. Inability to keep fluids down. \n\nSymptoms: Onset ~3 days ago; very loose watery stool progressing to frequent (today at least 4 movements); high fever felt hot to touch; multiple vomiting episodes; inability to retain oral fluids; abdominal pain localized to the periumbilical area, intermittent and cramping. \n\nPast medical history: Generally healthy aside from menstrual cramps; no chronic abdominal issues reported. \n\nMedications/allergies: Vitamin D supplements daily; occasional ibuprofen for cramps; no regular prescription meds; no reported drug allergies. \n\nFamily/social history: Lives with mother and brother; brother has similar symptoms and they are in a school setting with possible shared exposure; patient is a graphic designer; non-smoker; occasional alcohol use; no travel abroad reported. \n\nVital signs/exam findings: No numeric vitals provided. Clinician notes the fever as “seriously hot to touch.” Abdominal pain near the umbilicus; patient unable to palpate that area due to pain. Urine is being produced (urinating OK) with last urination ~1 hour ago. No blood in stool or urine reported. \n\nLabs/imaging: Plan to obtain a stool sample to check for bacterial infection; assessment of hydration status with urgent clinic review; stool testing and hydration monitoring are to be arranged. Advice given on oral rehydration and dietary precautions. \n\nImpression: Suspected infectious gastroenteritis (likely viral, could be bacterial) with dehydration risk; family exposure supports infectious etiology; no red flags for immediate surgical abdomen described in transcript.",
